================================================================================
INVARIANT - anything CX-backed
================================================================================
The CX layer resolves a ContextId to a module's user_data pointer ONCE and
reuses that same pointer. So a module must never move a surviving item's
storage once a ContextId has been handed out for it - no compaction on
remove; free slots become tombstones, reused only by a NEW item with a new
identity.

================================================================================
ENTITY PATTERN - params/categories are the reference implementation
================================================================================
Any new CX-backed object should follow this rather than re-deriving it:
1. Flat, tombstoned storage. Nesting is NOT nesting the storage.
2. uid minted by the owning module from a file-static, never-reset counter,
   so it is unique program-wide. 0 = "none" / "mint me one".
3. Opaque handle = pointer to the entity's own struct carrying self_container
   + self_local_id, plus a resolve function. See param_get_handle /
   param_handle_resolve and mirror it; do not invent a second convention.
4. Parent/child is a parent_uid FIELD on the entity - that is what makes a
   move a field write instead of a storage move.
5. app_data wraps each entity KIND in one DataOps table taking only the
   handle, so every owner shares it (cx_param_ops, cx_param_cat_ops).
6. ContextId = MAKE_ID(one namespace per KIND, uid). Per kind, never per
   owner - rule 2 makes uids program-unique.
7. Structural change = a generation counter on the store; app_data polls it
   and emits DATA_EVENT_CHILDREN_CHANGED; app_intrf diffs the whole subtree.

================================================================================
PLAN
================================================================================
1. PLUGIN PRESET BROWSING, with categories.
   Same shape as the existing "Add plugin" catalog list (a DataChoice list
   over list_count/list_at) but with category rows to drill into. Design is
   below, under DESIGN FOR (1).
   The clap module-level half exists already: clap_plug_presets_iterate /
   _name_return / _path_return / _categories_iterate / _clean_preset,
   clap_plug_preset_load_from_path, and the clap_ext_preset_factory
   extension. Missing is the DataObject side - a categorised choice list
   plus a load action.
   NAMING TRAP: clap_plug_presets_categories_iterate and friends are PRESET
   categories (a string path from the plugin's preset factory). Nothing to
   do with parameter categories (PRM_PARAM_CATEGORY). Same word, different
   mechanism.

2. CLAP + LV2 optimisation / cleanup. Owns the CLAP-vs-spec gaps listed
   under OPEN below. Fold anything found while doing (1) into it.

3. SAVING. Deliberately after (1) and (2): do not design the save format
   until what it must save has stopped moving - presets change what a
   plugin's restorable state even is, and the cleanup may change the param/
   state surface.
   NEVER serialise a params.c uid or a ContextId: the uid counter restarts
   at 0 every launch, so uid 7 is a different param next run. Save DOMAIN
   identity - plugin URI, the plugin's own param id (owner_id), sample path,
   port name. Every owner now supplies a real owner_id; LV2's is the control
   index and must be mapped to plug->controls[owner_id - 1]->symbol before
   writing, never persisted as the index.

4. CX_MOVED + reparenting. DEFERRED - no trigger yet.
   Only buys a non-destructive rename/move IN PLACE, which needs user-
   authored categories. NOT needed by CLAP rescan: a param changing category
   is already handled correctly as remove+add (see DECISIONS).
   If built: detection is nearly free from app_intrf_cx_resync's existing
   diff ("in both, different parent"); CxEvent needs no new field (a move
   carries the OLD parent/index, like CX_REMOVED); the hashtable entry does
   not change (keyed by data_id, which is what did not change); the apply
   order INVERTS to ADD -> MOVE -> REMOVE (additions first so a move's
   destination exists, removals last so survivors are moved out of doomed
   subtrees) - getting that backwards silently frees live nodes. ui_layer
   cost is ~3 lines: mark the view touched, no pruning (see DECISIONS).

5. SLOT-TABLE EXTRACTION. DEFERRED - no trigger yet.
   The tribal rules above (1-3) are not enforced anywhere; getting one wrong
   is a silent use-after-free. Extract into util_funcs/ when a THIRD growable
   identity-bearing entity appears (a pattern/clip list, a modulation matrix,
   user macros). Not before: the only two growable ones are params and
   categories, both in params.c, and they already disagree on whether removal
   exists - so which behaviour is "general" is not yet knowable.

================================================================================
DESIGN FOR (1) - categories in CHOICE / MULTI_CHOICE lists
================================================================================
No new DataListId/DataArgKind/DataActionType - still a flat, idx-enumerable
list_at.
- DataChoice gains DATA_CHOICE_CATEGORY (1u << 1) beside DATA_CHOICE_LINKED
  (1u << 0): a row with this bit is a node to descend into, not a leaf.
- DataActionReq gains a TOP-LEVEL field, sibling to `type`, outside the
  per-action union: `uint64_t category_scope;` (0 = root). Must not reuse a
  union field - add_choice.choice_value collides with CONNECT's usage.
- CLI drilling: one more branch in the existing "on SELECTED" handling that
  helper_choice_list_step's callers already have. A category row sets
  category_scope, resets the cursor, re-browses; a leaf behaves as today.
  Backing out needs a small caller-owned breadcrumb stack (scope, cursor)
  per level, capped like ACTION_ARG_COUNT - no heap.
- A category row's DataChoice.value must hash its FULL path, e.g.
  fnv1a64("Bass/Analog/Warm"), not just its own name, or two parents with a
  same-named child collide.
- list_at only ever needs the immediate parent's scope, not the ancestor
  chain.

================================================================================
OPEN
================================================================================
- CLI cannot collect a DATA_ARG_NUMBER arg. smp_groovebox_ui_cli.c's generic
  action-arg collection handles only DATA_ARG_PATH/DATA_ARG_CHOICE and falls
  through to "unsupported arguments". SET_VALUE/ADJUST_VALUE are fully wired
  and correctly gated at the DataOps layer but are unusable from the CLI.
  Needs CLI input work (digit accumulation, decimal point, sign, confirm/
  cancel) - not a DataObject or params.c change.
- CLAP vs the vendored clap/ext/params.h, for item (2):
  * CLAP_PARAM_IS_PERIODIC: no handling, undecided.
  * CLAP_PARAM_IS_BYPASS: empty stub, exposed as an ordinary param.
  * clap_output_events_read never parses a CLAP_EVENT_PARAM_VALUE the plugin
    sends, so plugin-side value changes never reach us.
  * Gesture BEGIN/END never wrapped around SET_VALUE/ADJUST_VALUE.
  * clap_host_params.request_flush()/flush() not audited - matters if a
    plugin is active while process() is not being called.
  * RESCAN_ALL survivor matching is by owner_id against the not-yet-pruned
    container: a plugin that frees a clap_id and reassigns it to an
    unrelated param within the SAME rescan gets the new param misidentified
    as a survivor, discarding its fresh name/min/max/default. Not fixed -
    params.c has no setter for a live param's min/max/inc and adding one
    raises reclamp-in-flight questions. Narrow; revisit if a real plugin
    hits it.
  * Automation/modulation (IS_AUTOMATABLE*/IS_MODULATABLE*, PER_NOTE_ID/
    PER_KEY/PER_CHANNEL/PER_PORT, CLAP_EVENT_PARAM_MOD): out of scope -
    automation needs a timeline engine this app lacks; modulation needs a
    routing concept related to but not reducible to CONNECT.
- Header hygiene: types.h has accumulated defines used by ONE module, which
  forces unrelated recompiles. MAX_CATEGORY_SEGMENT was moved to params.h
  for this reason. Walk the rest: anything referenced from a single .c/.h
  pair belongs in that pair's header; only genuinely cross-cutting things
  (PARAM_T, SAMPLE_T, MAX_SHORT_NAME_LENGTH) stay.

================================================================================
DECISIONS - settled, do not re-litigate
================================================================================
- user_data stays a borrowed POINTER, not a revalidatable key. A key
  ({owner, slot, uid} by value, re-resolved per call) would delete the
  INVARIANT above and make staleness detectable instead of UB - and it is
  already the pattern one layer down (PARAM_RING_DATA_BIT carries val_id AND
  uid for exactly that reason). Deferred anyway: whole-codebase refactor of
  every ops table, every accessor becomes a lookup, and params itself barely
  benefits since val_id stability is load-bearing for the rt side and Trk.
  Revisit only for data owned out of process / freed concurrently, a
  scripting layer defining its own tree, or UI state restored by identity
  across runs (and even then, serialising a path of names beats persisting
  ids).
- Param categories MINT uids; DataChoice category rows HASH their path. Not
  an inconsistency: a DataChoice row is stateless, enumerated on demand with
  no struct to hang identity off. Param categories are real entities with
  storage.
- A param changing category is handled as remove+add and that is CORRECT,
  not a degradation: a param has no children so there is no hover state
  inside it, and the old parent's hover pointing at it SHOULD re-anchor,
  having genuinely left that list. Categories themselves never move under a
  rescan - interning matches by (parent, name), so a changed module path
  interns a new chain and the old entity just goes empty.
- Empty categories are not garbage-collected. The interned entry lingers
  (a pointer + a name buffer, freed with the container); the CX disappears
  because the enumerator stops returning it. Its hover entry goes with it,
  which is correct - a category with no params is genuinely gone.
- TARGET-LIST CONTRACT: ui_layer's only hard obligation is that no stored
  ContextId ever names a DESTROYED context. Beyond that a view may store
  what it likes; a stale-but-valid id self-heals (helper_nav_context_single_
  purpose_set scans the parent's live children and falls back to index 0).
  What makes that safe is DataOps.id's "never reused for a different object".
  UiStalePolicy modes are ergonomics, not safety.
- is_hidden/value_as_string/value_range stay their own DataOps entries
  rather than generic "properties": they are read on hot paths or gate
  behaviour. DATA_CAP_PROPERTIES was built for the param category and
  removed with it - it had no consumer.
